 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 2,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "NORWICH. A fine city, say the signs on every road in. Once the second city of England, built on wool, worsted and weaving. West to the Fens, east to the Broads."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "NORWICH CATHEDRAL. Begun in 1096, of Caen stone brought from Normandy by sea and river. Its spire is the second tallest in England, and its cloisters are the largest of any monastery in the land.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_norwich_cathedral",
   "title": "Norwich Cathedral"
  },
  {
   "x": 59,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "PULL'S FERRY. The old water gate by the river. Here a canal once ran up to the cathedral, so boats could unload Normandy stone right beside the building site. Named after John Pull, who ran the ferry in the 1800s.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_pulls_ferry",
   "title": "Pull's Ferry"
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "NORWICH CASTLE. A Norman keep on a great earth mound, finished by about 1121. Royal palace, then the county gaol for six centuries, then a museum since 1894. Kett the rebel was hanged from its walls in 1549.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_norwich_castle",
   "people": "normans",
   "title": "Norwich Castle"
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "NORWICH MARKET. There has been a market here since the Normans laid it out in the 11th century. Today it's one of the largest open-air markets in England, under rows of stripy roofs. Get the chips.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_norwich_market",
   "title": "Norwich Market"
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "ELM HILL. A cobbled medieval street of timber-framed houses, most rebuilt after the great fire of 1507. It nearly got knocked down in the 1920s. The Norwich Society talked the council out of it, by the narrowest of votes, so the story goes.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_elm_hill",
   "title": "Elm Hill"
  },
  {
   "x": 9,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "JULIAN OF NORWICH. An anchoress who lived in a cell at St Julian's Church and wrote Revelations of Divine Love, the earliest surviving book in English known to be by a woman. All shall be well.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_julian",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Julian of Norwich"
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 32,
   "text": "THE STRANGERS. In 1565 Norwich invited Protestant weavers from Flanders and the Low Countries to settle. They brought new cloths, new skills and their songbirds: canaries. Within a generation, about a third of the city spoke Dutch or French.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_strangers",
   "people": "huguenots",
   "title": "The Strangers"
  },
  {
   "x": 65,
   "y": 46,
   "text": "THE CANARIES. Norwich City wear yellow and green after the canaries bred here since the Strangers came. The club took the name in 1907. Their song, On the Ball, City, dates from the 1890s.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_canaries",
   "title": "The Canaries"
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 41,
   "text": "FLINT. With no good building stone, Norfolk builds in flint from the chalk, knapped to show its glassy black heart, with brick for corners. Patterns of flint and stone are called flushwork.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_flint",
   "title": "Norfolk Flint"
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 32,
   "text": "A CHURCH FOR EVERY WEEK AND A PUB FOR EVERY DAY, they used to say of Norwich. Thirty-one medieval churches still stand inside the old city walls, more than in any other city north of the Alps.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_churches",
   "title": "Churches and Pubs"
  },
  {
